    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 10,
        "sourceEnd": 147,
        "text": "You are listening to a seminar discussion. The speakers are Dr. Helen Marsh, a lecturer in urban studies, and two students, Wei and Anna.",
        "boundary": "section",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "63e8103eab7ef29332326ff4f46dfea73e91a21108ca928fff75c71cbd451e64",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 149,
        "sourceEnd": 344,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: Good morning. Today we ask a simple but uncomfortable question: who really gains when a city regenerates an old district? Wei, what does the word 'regeneration' usually suggest to you?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "c5d4d947a2a8f427f3d7246cdd0076991af5072122def3167e948223ff0c7814",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 351,
        "sourceEnd": 476,
        "text": "I think it suggests improvement. New shops, better transport, cleaner streets. But I read that some residents are pushed out.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "7aeaab79248c8df502791c0c922123ad915b01ca4e764c03294c25ae82bd81a8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 478,
        "sourceEnd": 636,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: Exactly. Regeneration is often presented as a benefit for everyone, but the benefits are rarely shared equally. Anna, what did the case study show?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "bc9822748e2722096dc39fd928179b0592a6f49305ad61fc571fb79316f873ce",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "anna",
        "speaker": "ANNA",
        "sourceStart": 644,
        "sourceEnd": 852,
        "text": "The case study was about a riverside area. The council invested in a new park and a footbridge. Property prices rose by about thirty percent in three years. Some local families could no longer pay their rent.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "b54ce8eb195c9ffff40a8d4e41575021eb9058dbb9f3a748f4e64add2189b97a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 854,
        "sourceEnd": 964,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: So the park was a public good, but it also increased private land values. Who captured those gains?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "df79a8deeef30f83414d7a13e35484268240b567f78db0961342cb78c562c860",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "anna",
        "speaker": "ANNA",
        "sourceStart": 972,
        "sourceEnd": 1075,
        "text": "Mostly the landowners and the developers. The families who had lived there for years did not gain much.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "8f670171f0767f7c15d77ab163f07f23fef3b9746fbb4eade473d28d6161e3de",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 1082,
        "sourceEnd": 1160,
        "text": "But surely the new footbridge helped people commute? That is a gain, isn't it?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "5bb32085afce55e100935aa48f3ca97d3313292db83f76492df944a485cbaf7e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1162,
        "sourceEnd": 1399,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: It can be. The question is whether the gain reaches the people who need it most. If the footbridge mainly serves new luxury apartments, then the public money has subsidised private profit. That is the tension we are examining.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "065f07131b26931c103ca61ecd3b118b7a745fcef96d94c3bfc15658a9f0c2d0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 1406,
        "sourceEnd": 1441,
        "text": "So should cities stop regenerating?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "e63895038666e017569d9f9c116b6fbb36fa73b74dd325a7adf89ea1157ee099",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1443,
        "sourceEnd": 1676,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: No, that is too simple. The point is not to stop investment but to ask who pays and who benefits. Some cities now require developers to include affordable housing. Others give residents a formal role in planning decisions.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "db63662d103842d6065523ee4a86b3fb748dd1fb4ad8bff64b3f4dbc7f904246",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "anna",
        "speaker": "ANNA",
        "sourceStart": 1684,
        "sourceEnd": 1757,
        "text": "In the case study, was there any compensation for the displaced families?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "a8675a302305b215a5074eb821eb041a5b44005664f77b95c1d031db646882c8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1759,
        "sourceEnd": 1946,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: There was a small relocation fund, but it was described as limited and difficult to access. The report said many families did not apply because the process was too complicated.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "875b6a3d546682d9a9c39cc12da180a83298b9f2bc8e0bf5a4d59edfd0958b67",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 1953,
        "sourceEnd": 2013,
        "text": "That sounds like a design problem, not just a money problem.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "9fe5fdc5d052ca65279c8cce3800b71db5b40d11d178e748342b0a860009e481",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2015,
        "sourceEnd": 2135,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: Good point. Access matters as much as the amount. A benefit that people cannot reach is not really a benefit.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "28821b95b2fbc61cdaf79e4ab650a00e356385112f286c109929d2057ce333b1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "anna",
        "speaker": "ANNA",
        "sourceStart": 2143,
        "sourceEnd": 2268,
        "text": "I noticed the report used passive sentences a lot. 'The area was regenerated.' 'Funds were allocated.' It hides who did what.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "97b08256ef2597d1ff901f8169e222e57d27af9fb9460e010401adc0bbf947c6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2270,
        "sourceEnd": 2577,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: That is a very useful observation. Passive structures can be appropriate when the actor is unknown or unimportant. But in political texts, they can also avoid responsibility. Compare 'The council closed the centre' with 'The centre was closed'. The second sentence does not tell us who closed it.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "9065a5249293accfbea71f8b9c32ac9993e24997460f4b6c93102ae220e37590",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 2584,
        "sourceEnd": 2656,
        "text": "So when we read reports, we should ask: who is the actor? Who is hidden?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "f8a58365c4f50e7338cd56a2808852808456b2c40062016669ace266dbc161fb",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2658,
        "sourceEnd": 2818,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: Yes. And in your own writing, use the passive when the action matters more than the actor, but use active sentences when responsibility is the point.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "bb74990f650d91d7323feaa37ef6e106fdd16c5c010926a3245011f5240ca992",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "anna",
        "speaker": "ANNA",
        "sourceStart": 2826,
        "sourceEnd": 2987,
        "text": "One more thing. The report said the new park was 'welcomed by all'. But our interviews showed that older residents felt the park was designed for young families.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "17e62aed5fe047badcce6148b569260e1d44f7887ce5f34b5a64fd39eb90ab04",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2989,
        "sourceEnd": 3158,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: That is a classic gap between official language and lived experience. 'Welcomed by all' is a claim, not evidence. Always ask: welcomed by whom? Based on what?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "8d13d4edd865f50dff8c595efb7eb72d7dad2dfaefa739df8ce0484d74874fe0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "wei",
        "speaker": "WEI",
        "sourceStart": 3165,
        "sourceEnd": 3272,
        "text": "So the seminar question is not just 'did regeneration happen?' but 'who gained, who lost, and who decided?'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "6490291210303a3cf85110fb2b2bd2fff252b415129091bc134f912745f655de",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 3274,
        "sourceEnd": 3482,
        "text": "DR. MARSH: Precisely. For next week, bring one example of regeneration language from a local news article. Identify the actors, the passive structures, and the groups who are mentioned and missing. Thank you.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-BrianNeural",
        "textHash": "b753f04abc250270b7992f2282f77d0e1994341229b17245fffa8b50f47e9c3c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
